<section class="k-sec tight k-beige"><div class="k-wrap"><div class="b-art"><article class="k-prose"><p>A job interview is often seen as a test you have to pass, when it's just as much a chance to test whether a company fits you. You can't read company culture from a vacancy full of words like “informal atmosphere” and “flat organisation”; they say little. You spot it in a handful of concrete signals during the conversation itself.</p>
<h2>Can you really judge company culture in one thirty-minute interview?</h2><p>Not completely, but partially. You won't get a full picture, but you'll get signals: how people talk about colleagues, how they react to a critical question, whether it's a conversation or a one-sided interrogation. Those signals give more information than the marketing language on a website.</p>
<h2>What concrete signals should you look for during the interview?</h2><ul><li>Does the interviewer talk about the team with genuine enthusiasm, or mostly in general, rehearsed terms?</li><li>Do they describe what a normal working day or week looks like, or does it stay at abstract words like “dynamic”?</li><li>Is there room for your questions, or does the conversation feel one-sided?</li><li>Does the interviewer respond calmly to a critical question, or evasively?</li></ul>
<h2>What questions get you past the standard answers?</h2><p>Don't ask “what's the culture like here”, because that almost always gets a rehearsed answer. Ask something concrete: “What did the last working student here actually do in their first month?” or “Can you tell me about a time something didn't go to plan, and how the team handled it?”. Concrete questions force concrete answers instead of marketing lines.</p>
<h2>What does it mean if a company can't give a concrete answer?</h2><p>A company that has really thought through a working-student role can tell you what the first month looks like, who the point of contact is and what a normal working week involves. If the answer stays vague, the role may not be properly thought through yet. Ask follow-up questions before you sign.</p>
<h2>How much weight should an interview carry against reviews or word of mouth?</h2><p>Combine them. An interview gives you a direct impression, reviews give you a pattern over time, and someone who works or has worked there gives you the most honest picture. None of the three is complete, but together they paint a far more realistic picture than the vacancy alone.</p>
